; `(< s 1.)` compares a vec2f against a scalar — no such overload exists, so
; inference pushes f32 into `s` before the threaded binding value (whose
; type arrives late, through the global) resolves to vec2f. The resulting
; conflict is attributed to the binding value: the `let` scaffolding
; synthesized by the `->` macro. Errors on macro-generated nodes must still
; describe with a real source position (the `->` form's) instead of
; crashing error reporting; the
; `thread_macro_error_describes_with_source_position` test pins this.

@uniform
(var dims: vec2f)

(defn f []: bool
  (let [s (-> dims
              (/ <> (min (.x <>) (.y <>))))]
    (< s 1.)))
